## 4. Architecture Comparison

| Approach | Compatibility | Risk to Device | Reversibility | Time to Demo | Practical Usefulness | Verdict |
|----------|--------------|----------------|---------------|--------------|---------------------|---------|
| **Android-assisted chroot (Alpine/Debian armel)** | High — uses existing kernel/drivers | Very Low — no partition writes | High — delete rootfs image | Days to 1–2 weeks | High — real Linux tools | **Recommended** |
| **PRoot (no root required)** | High — userspace only | None | Trivial | Days | Medium — slower, some limitations | Viable fallback |
| **DebDroid / Linux Deploy (existing tools)** | Medium — may assume newer kernel | Low | High | Days | Medium — may not support ARMv6 | Test as shortcut |
| **Specialized Android app (Termux, etc.)** | High | None | N/A | Hours | Medium — limited package availability | Viable for quick win |
| **Custom kernel + independent boot** | Low — requires kernel port | High — boot partition writes | Low — brick risk | Months to years | Potentially high, but unproven | **Defer/Abandon** |
| **Mainline Linux port** | Very Low — no support exists | High | Low | Years | Theoretically high | **Abandon** |
| **Custom minimal rootfs (LFS/Buildroot)** | Medium — full control | Low | High | Weeks | Medium — more work than Alpine | Not recommended first |

### Detailed assessment

**Android-assisted chroot** is the clear winner. It requires no kernel changes, no partition writes, and no bootloader modification. The repository has already proven the two critical mechanics: `chroot()` works, and loop-mounted ext2 works. The remaining work is userspace: obtaining an ARMv6-compatible rootfs, testing binary execution, and installing tools. The risk is nearly zero—if something fails, you delete the image file and the phone is unchanged.

**PRoot** is worth mentioning as a fallback if chroot execution proves problematic for dynamic binaries. PRoot is a userspace implementation of `chroot`, `mount --bind`, and `binfmt_misc` that requires no root privileges. It is slower than real chroot because it intercepts syscalls via `ptrace`, but it may work when real chroot fails due to kernel limitations. For a device with this old a kernel, PRoot is a pragmatic safety net.

**Existing Android chroot tools** like Linux Deploy, DebDroid, and Complete Linux Installer are worth testing as shortcuts. However, DebDroid explicitly requires kernel 3.10 or newer (released December 2017), which the Totoro does not meet. Linux Deploy supports Android 2.1+, which is more promising, but its ARMv6 support is uncertain. These tools should be tested quickly, but the project should not depend on them.

**Independent Linux boot** is the most ambitious path and the least justified by evidence. The BCM21553 has no mainline kernel support. The Dissonant wiki page on BCM215xx is a stub created in April 2026 with no actual mainlining progress documented—it merely lists development boards and Samsung devices. The XDA threads from 2012 describing "Linux native boot" are unfinished experiments from over a decade ago, and the developers who attempted them have moved on. There is no active community, no device tree, no hardware documentation, and no realistic path to a working independent Linux system on this hardware. Pursuing this would be a research project, not an engineering project.

**Custom kernel development** is equally problematic. While custom kernels exist (Merruk, White, Watson, Samurai), they are all Android-oriented kernels designed to improve Android performance, not to enable independent Linux boot. The Merruk kernel mentions "Linux native boot (done but not perfect)" but this is from 2012 and was never completed. Building on this would mean inheriting a decade-old codebase with unknown bugs and no upstream support.

