## 8. Premature or Unnecessary Work

### Independent Linux boot — DEFER (effectively abandon)

**Evidence against:** No mainline kernel support. No device tree. No hardware documentation. No active community. The only documented attempt (XDA 2012) was never completed. The Dissonant wiki page is an empty stub created in 2026 with no actual mainlining progress. The effort required is measured in person-years, and the probability of success is very low. The payoff—a device that boots Linux independently but has no better hardware support than Android provides—does not justify the risk and effort.

**Recommendation:** Formally close this path. Document it as "investigated and rejected for this hardware." Do not spend further time on it unless a full mainline port appears from another party.

### Custom kernel development — DEFER

**Evidence against:** All existing custom kernels (Merruk, White, Watson, Samurai) are Android-oriented. They do not enable independent Linux boot. They are from 2012–2016 and have no upstream support. Building a custom kernel would mean inheriting a decade-old codebase, patching it for modern userspace compatibility, and debugging unknown issues—without any guarantee that independent boot would work even then.

**Recommendation:** Do not build a custom kernel unless a specific, verified kernel feature is missing that is required for the chroot to function. Even then, prefer working around the limitation rather than changing the kernel.

### Mainline Linux porting — ABANDON

**Evidence against:** The BCM21553 is a 2010-era ARM11 SoC with no mainline support. The effort to add support—reverse-engineering the SoC, writing device tree bindings, implementing clock/power/display/storage drivers—is enormous and has no community backing. This is a research project, not an engineering project.

### Distribution selection before compatibility testing — PREMATURE

**Evidence against:** The repository has not yet verified that dynamic ELF binaries can execute. Selecting Alpine vs. Debian vs. SliTaz before this test is premature. The right order is: (1) test dynamic execution, (2) test package manager, (3) test specific tools, (4) choose the distribution that works.

**Recommendation:** Download both Alpine armhf and Debian armel minirootfs tarballs. Test both. Choose based on empirical results, not theory.

### GUI desktop investigation — PREMATURE

**Evidence against:** The 290 MB RAM budget and 240×320 screen make a GUI desktop impractical for anything beyond a demonstration. The effort to set up X11/VNC is not justified until the terminal-based environment is working and the user has a concrete need for GUI.

**Recommendation:** Defer all GUI work until Milestone 3 is complete and the user explicitly requests it.

