## What I would validate first—and why

**The single highest-value next experiment is to test whether a dynamically linked ARMv6 Linux binary can execute in a chroot on the Totoro.**

This is the critical path. Everything else depends on this answer. If dynamic binaries work, the project can proceed with Alpine or Debian armel, install packages, run SSHd, and deliver a genuinely useful Linux environment. If they don't work, the entire architecture of the project must change—falling back to PRoot, static binaries, or abandoning the Linux-userspace approach entirely.

The experiment is low-risk (writes only to a test directory on the SD card), fast (can be completed in an hour), and unambiguous (either `busybox` runs or it doesn't). It answers the one question that determines whether the project's current direction is viable or needs a fundamental pivot.

**Procedure:** Download the Alpine Linux minirootfs armhf tarball, extract it to `/sdcard/chroot-test/`, enter the chroot as root, and run `/bin/busybox echo "test"` followed by `/sbin/apk --version`. If both commands produce output, the path forward is clear. If either fails, the project must immediately reassess its architecture before investing further effort.

Do this before writing any more documentation, planning any more milestones, or investigating any other technical direction. The answer to this single question determines the shortest safe path to a useful Totoro.
